  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Increasing winds continue today, especially across northwest \n  Montana and the higher terrain. \n\n- Scattered rain showers tonight into early Wednesday then slight\n  warming trend into the remainder of the week.\n\n- High confidence in warmer and drier conditions this weekend\n  through much of next week (and maybe beyond).\n\n\nSurface analysis as of early this afternoon indicates a broad \narea of low pressure over the Gulf of Alaska and an area of high \npressure off the coast of California. Satellite imagery shows\nthese two features forcing a plume of moisture onshore into\nWashington and southern British Columbia. The plume will reach the\nnorthern half of the Northern Rockies this evening and overnight \ntonight. While moisture will be available, forcing for ascent will\nbe scarce as the upper level jet remains north of the border.\nPrecipitation tonight through midday Wednesday will remain \npredominantly terrain-forced, though isolated light showers may \nspill into adjacent valleys. Valley rainfall totals will remain \nminimal (a few hundredths of an inch at most), while mountain \nlocations across northwest Montana can expect 0.10 to 0.30 inches.\n\nUpper level flow on Wednesday becomes west-northwesterly. The\nupper level jet will sag south of the border, bringing increasing\nwest winds to the higher terrain of western Montana including the\nIdaho-Montana border.   \n\nHigh confidence (80-90% ensemble cluster support) exists for \nstrong upper-level ridging to build over the Northern Rockies this\nweekend and persist into next week. While 850mb temperatures \nsupport daytime highs 10-20 degrees above seasonal averages, \nvalley temperatures may be slightly tempered by autumn valley \ninversions and limited boundary-layer mixing. Conversely, \novernight valley lows are expected to trend cooler than blended \nguidance such as the NBM suggests, as dry air, light winds, and \nclear skies facilitate efficient nocturnal radiational cooling. \nIt's interesting to note that many ensembles point toward warmer \nand drier than normal conditions continuing through the entire \nfirst half of October.",
